Here is what I found others doing. I fetched each link marked checked;
the rest came from search results only.

- Karpathy's "LLM Wiki" gist [a] (checked) has three layers. Raw
  sources the LLM never edits, a wiki/ the LLM owns, and a schema file
  (CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md) that governs it. Its operations are ingest
  (one source updates many pages), query (answers cite pages and can
  be filed back), and a periodic lint for contradictions, stale claims
  and orphan pages. index.md catalogs it and log.md is an append-only
  changelog.
- Simon Willison's research repo [b][c] (checked) runs one folder per
  question for async coding agents. He keeps the output quarantined
  in its own repo, whose README says every line was written by an LLM,
  so it never mixes with his own writing.
- Elicit Routines [d] (checked) run daily or weekly, revise a standing
  artifact such as an evidence summary, and report what changed with
  source links. ChatGPT scheduled tasks [e] (not checked; the page
  refused my fetch) do the recurring part without a standing artifact.
- Stanford STORM and Co-STORM [f] (checked) research, outline, then
  write a cited article. Co-STORM keeps a mind map as the shared
  structure between the human and the system.
- The living literature review work [g] (checked) is the warning. A
  later paper, "Toward Living Narrative Reviews" (CHI '25, not
  checked), reports that most living reviews are never updated. A
  "living" document dies without a mechanism that forces the update.
- Zettelkasten literature notes versus permanent notes [h], and
  Matuschak's separate reading and writing inboxes [i] (both checked),
  are the pre-LLM form of the same split you describe: material about
  sources in one place, your own thinking in another.

For software factories specifically, two trackers already exist that
an agent could watch rather than rediscover. Addy Osmani's "Software
Factories, Light and Dark" [j] (checked) frames the space as harnessed
loops plus a review gate. The awesome-software-factories list [k]
(checked) is a community tracker by category, with markers for
unmaintained and discontinued projects.

The patterns that recur across all of them:

1. Sources are kept apart from synthesis, and agent text apart from
   human text. One author per file.
2. A schema file is the contract, so every run behaves the same way.
3. Updates are deltas on a fixed cadence, "what changed since last
   time", not a regenerated report.
4. Every claim carries provenance.
5. A maintenance pass hunts stale claims, or the document rots.

