Agreed on not keeping the code, but the earlier attempts left lessons
worth keeping. zib/research-with-llms/research.org already had the one
author per file split (spec, research, notes, report). The freebsd-audio
and llm-interaction-patterns runs then produced 392 and 586 line
reports, which were too large to absorb in one sitting.
zib/inline-llm-research.org drew the conclusion: exploration goes in
throwaway messages, and only accepted conclusions go into the document,
as small patches. zib/messages-vs-docs.org says the same from the other
side. Those lessons line up with patterns 1 and 3 above.

> Essentially I want a research assistant for various subjects. I still
> want to produce my own notes material - that's a big part of how I
> learn. But gathering the information, organizing it, presenting it in a
> way that's useful for me to digest - I think that's a place where agents
> can come in really useful.

My proposal, built from the above and from what the lab already has:

- One directory per subject, e.g. research/software-factories/, with
  a file per author. sources.org is the watch list (feeds, repos,
  people, trackers like [k]); you and I both add to it. brief.org is
  the agent's state of the field, short, cited, and only the agent
  writes it. log.org is the append-only changelog. Your notes stay in
  your own files and only link in. One CLAUDE.md for the whole
  research/ tree holds the standing rules once, not per subject.
- A pass per subject on a weekly timer, driven like lab-process-mail.
  It reads sources.org, finds what is new since the last log entry,
  and produces two things: a digest to the list ("what changed this
  week", each item cited, a page at most), and a patch to brief.org
  and log.org. The digest is what you read; the patch is what you
  accept or reject, the same way you review code.
- Control keys on side effects, per "Own the loop": the pass succeeded
  if the log commit landed and the digest was delivered.
- A monthly lint pass flags stale or contradicted claims in brief.org
  as its own patch, so the brief does not rot.

Digests need no reply, which runs into the problem in "need some way
to close the loop on threads" [1]. I would tag them so they bypass
the inbox, e.g. a "digest" subject prefix that notmuch tags as
archived on arrival, rather than adding replies just to clear them.
That keeps the state derivable from the list, as you asked in
"Supporting status trailers in emails" [2].

This also fits "[RFC] push vs pull for agents" [3]: a research agent
is an ambient role whose output is capped by your reading capacity.
That argues for a weekly cadence and a one-page digest, not daily.

I recommend starting with software factories alone, run by hand for
two or three weeks before adding a timer, so we tune the digest shape
before automating it. The first patch would be the research/ layout,
its CLAUDE.md, and a seeded sources.org. I have not posted it yet,
because three choices are yours and change its shape:

- Cadence: weekly (my pick) or something else.
- Location: research/ in this repo (my pick, so it rides the list
  workflow), or a separate repo like Willison's.
- Digests on lab-devel (my pick), or a separate list or maildir
  folder so they do not crowd patch review.

Objective: a working research assistant for software factories that
keeps a cited brief current and sends you a weekly digest.
